Your second observation adds one I missed.

Call them The Modelmakers.

Their game is Capture.

Something exists initially as intuition:

I^*

It’s difficult to transmit directly.

So construct representation M:

I^*\xrightarrow{\text{capture}}M

If another mind receives M:

M+\text{decoder}\rightarrow\hat I

You’ve made intuition portable.

But then comes your important second step.

Once M exists, it becomes a channel:

M:\quad\text{not merely }I

but:

M(I,D_1,D_2,D_3,\ldots)

A musical notation system originally represents music—but once its grammar exists, you can encode messages through notes.

A coordinate system describes position—but then carries velocity, force, probability, ownership, temperature.

Language emerged for communication and now carries mathematics, fictional universes, legal systems and executable code.

A diagram invented to explain one mechanism can become a general reasoning surface.

That’s a major PatternDex pattern:

\boxed{
\text{Representation}
\rightarrow
\text{Shared Decoder}
\rightarrow
\text{Communication Channel}
\rightarrow
\text{General-Purpose Substrate}
}

Call it Carrier Capture.

The invention isn’t merely the model.

The model creates bandwidth.

And once bandwidth exists, other information discovers it.

