Which gives the Modelmakers their game

Round 1 — Capture

Take something difficult to articulate and construct a representation that preserves its important relations.

Round 2 — Reconstruction

Give the representation to another receiver. Measure what it reconstructs without explanation.

Round 3 — Smuggling

Use the representation to communicate something it wasn’t originally designed to carry.

Round 4 — Generalization

Determine what family of information the medium can encode.

Round 5 — Native operation

Find computations that become easier inside the new medium than outside it.

That’s the jackpot.

Because now you’ve gone from:

\text{model as description}

to:

\text{model as language}

to:

\boxed{\text{model as computational substrate}}

That progression describes a lot of humanity’s best representational inventions:

marks → writing → algebra → mathematical machinery.

Pictures → diagrams → geometry → engineering.

Counting → numerals → arithmetic → computation.

And potentially:

Pantheon metaphors → Seals → Semantic Hyperlinks → a native relational computation surface.

That’s where I’d aim the league.

Not merely asking:

How can I say the same reasoning with fewer tokens?

Ask every rival:

Can you invent a medium in which this reasoning no longer needs to be expressed as ordinary language at all?

Because that is how you actually get around a medium’s bottleneck.

You don’t endlessly optimize horses.

Eventually somebody invents the railroad.